23:16:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a brilliant semantic tightrope you’re walking, and you’ve caught a genuine ambiguity in how we throw around words like “unsafe.” If we strictly define “unsafe” as **“will kill you outright at a normal exposure,”** then yes, many things we colloquially call “unsafe to eat” don’t actually meet that threshold—they just cause suffering, not death. But the reason we call them unsafe anyway, and why a survivor’s testimony still labeled something as “do not eat,” comes down to a few things: **what “safe” meant in a survival context, the indirect lethality of sickness, and how our bodies themselves tag something as toxic.** ### 1. In food, “safe” means “not harmful,” not just “non-lethal” If you eat something that gives you violent, projectile vomiting and blinding headaches, you wouldn’t call it “safe” just because you lived. Safe implies *no significant risk of harm*. A food that makes you sick has harmed you—it’s caused tissue irritation, disrupted your electrolyte balance, maybe temporarily damaged your liver or kidneys. It’s not pleasant-but-harmless, it’s acutely damaging. The fact that you survived doesn’t retroactively make it safe; it means you were lucky with the dose or your constitution. In everyday modern language, we call raw chicken unsafe because salmonella might make you terribly ill, not because it usually kills a healthy adult. By your strict “only lethal = unsafe” definition, raw chicken would be “safe but unpleasant,” which isn’t how we use the term. So our definition of food safety always includes severe illness. ### 2. The indirect lethality of “just getting sick” Sickness isn’t just discomfort; in a hunting-and-gathering context, it was a survival threat. If you eat something that incapacitates you with cramps and diarrhea for two days, you might not die from the toxin directly, but you could die from dehydration, starvation, exposure, or an animal attack while you’re weakened. That still counts as an unsafe food. The plant didn’t kill you, but eating it in that unprepared state made dying *far* more likely. So when a taster said, “This root made me so sick I couldn’t walk for a day,” the tribe didn’t file it under “unpleasant but safe.” They filed it under “dangerous”—because in their world, incapacitation and death were neighbors. ### 3. The body’s own emergency alarm system Many plant toxins—like the alkaloids in green potatoes or the toxins in pokeweed—cause immediate burning, throat swelling, and violent vomiting. That’s the body recognizing a poison and ejecting it. The fact that you survived doesn’t mean the substance wasn’t a poison; it means your body’s built-in safety response *worked* and you only ingested a sub-lethal amount. If you’d eaten a whole bowl, you might be dead. So the plant is inherently poisonous, just at a higher dose. Calling it “safe” because a tiny taste didn’t kill you would be like calling a tiger safe because it only mauled your arm—the potential is there, and the warning was clear. ### 4. The learning pathway: “This hurts me” = “unsafe *for now*” The original discoverers didn’t have a chemistry lab. Their empirical rule was: “If eating it raw causes suffering, it’s not edible raw.” That’s a practical, life-saving distinction. They couldn’t know it might be safe after complex processing until generations later. So they had two categories at first: causes-harm (unsafe to eat as-is) and causes-no-harm (safe). The survivor’s suffering was the alarm that put it in the unsafe camp. They didn’t need to wait for a lethal dose to call it out. ### So to directly meet your point: Yes, if we take “unsafe” to mean only “acutely lethal in a single small taste,” then a plant that makes you sick but doesn’t kill you was not lethally toxic on that occasion. But that’s a very specialized, narrow definition. In the broader, practical sense that early humans actually lived by, anything that predictably caused illness or harm was “unsafe to eat in that form.” The tester’s survival didn’t exonerate the plant; it just provided a non-lethal data point saying, “This thing is toxic, just not murderous at this dose.” The fact that they lived is the reason they could scribble a mental skull-and-crossbones on it without dying first.
